# `__modname__` command group

`weld add command __modname__` generated this package. weld writes it once and
records every file in `weld.json`; it does not regenerate them, so your edits
survive later `weld add` commands.

## What it is

An independent root command group that starts no long-running work. `NewCommand`
returns the `__modname__` command; with no subcommands a bare
`__name__ __modname__` shows help. The generated `status` subcommand is the
worked example of a subcommand that reads an injected dependency.

Generating the command also installed `loom` (and the `config` it requires), so
the group resolves its dependencies from the command-specific graph in
`internal/di/__modname___graph.go`. That graph includes `commonModule`, the
shared provider set `di.go` declares (configuration, logger and the installed
db/redis/mail/storage bindings), plus the `Deps` root, and Loom prunes every
provider a subcommand does not consume, so the group never constructs the HTTP
server, the scheduler or a database pool.

## How it is wired

`internal/app/__modname___command.go` registers the group through
`app.RegisterCommand` and delegates `Build` to `di.Init__ModName__`. A real
subcommand calls `commandkit.Run`, which builds and starts the graph for that one
invocation and stops it within a bounded deadline; help, `version` and every
unrelated command never resolve it. `internal/commandkit` is the shared seam and
the command package never imports Loom itself.

## Injecting a dependency

`Deps` is the root of the command's graph: one field per dependency a subcommand
needs, and one matching parameter on `NewDeps`. There are two cases:

- A dependency the shared `commonModule` already provides needs nothing else —
  `*config.Config`, `*slog.Logger` and, when installed, `data.Repository` and the
  Redis, mail and storage bindings. The `status` subcommand's `Deps.Logger` is
  the worked example: adding the field and the `NewDeps` parameter is all it
  takes, and Loom injects it.
- A dependency no provider declares needs a provider in
  `internal/di/__modname___graph.go`, for example `loom.Provide(NewThing)`, with
  the same field here and the same parameter on `NewDeps`. A capability
  installed after this command still reaches it, because the shared providers
  live in `commonModule`.

`internal/di/loom_gen.go` is generated by Loom; never edit it.

## Adding behaviour

Add subcommands in `command.go` with `root.AddCommand`. Each real subcommand
resolves the graph lazily through `commandkit.Run`, for example:

```go
RunE: func(cmd *cobra.Command, args []string) error {
    return commandkit.Run(cmd.Context(), build, func(ctx context.Context, deps *Deps) error {
        // use ctx and deps, then return nil
        return nil
    })
}
```
